    {
      "id": "171",
      "headline": "Man Spends 47 Years Learning To Say No, Finally Masters It, Immediately Says Yes To Everything Again",
      "summary": "Gary learned assertiveness, forgot why he wanted to say no, and is now back to 'sure, I'd love to.'",
      "fullBody": "Gary Yesman, 52, has spent 47 years trying to learn how to say no, and after completing an assertiveness course, he finally mastered the skill. The course took 47 weeks, during which Gary practiced saying no to his wife, his boss, his children, and random strangers asking for directions. At the graduation ceremony, Gary demonstrated his newfound ability by saying no to the instructor's handshake, no to the certificate presentation, and no to a complimentary coffee. However, within 24 hours, Gary had said yes to 47 requests, including 'will you work this weekend,' 'can I borrow $500,' and 'please drive my kid to soccer practice again.' Gary said, 'I think I forgot why I wanted to say no. Everyone just seemed so nice when they asked.' He is now enrolled in a 'Saying No, Part 2' course.",
      "category": "lifestyle",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/171.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Man saying NO in mirror with confident expression, then immediately saying YES to person with pleading eyes, comic illustration, flat design, blue and green colors",
      "publishedAt": "21 hrs ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 74,
      "tags": ["Lifestyle", "Assertiveness", "No", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "172",
      "headline": "New App 'Mood Tracker' That Only Records Negative Emotions, Calls It 'Realistic Mental Health'",
      "summary": "The 'HonestMind' app deletes all positive entries because 'happiness is just denial with a smile.'",
      "fullBody": "Anti-Sugarcoating Mental Health Technologies has released 'HonestMind,' a mood tracking app that only records negative emotions and deletes anything positive because 'happiness is just denial with a smile.' The app sends weekly reports showing 'Your week was mostly terrible with occasional moments of not being terrible, here's the breakdown.' One user said, 'I tried to log 'feeling good' and the app said 'That's not a real emotion, try again with something authentic.' I logged 'mildly okay' instead.' The app has been praised for 'finally being honest about mental health' and criticized by therapists who say 'we need to track the good things too.' The company responds with 'the good things don't need tracking, they need to be questioned.'",
      "category": "lifestyle",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/172.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Mood tracking app showing chart with only negative emotions, positive entries being deleted, person looking at report saying MOSTLY TERRIBLE, comic illustration, flat design, gray and purple colors",
      "publishedAt": "22 hrs ago",
