      "id": "177",
      "headline": "New Restaurant Concept: Diners Pay Based On How Much They Apologize During Meal",
      "summary": "The 'Sorry Cafe' charges $5 per apology, and customers who say 'Sorry' more than 20 times eat for free.",
      "fullBody": "The Sorry Cafe has opened in Chicago with a unique pricing model: customers pay based on how many times they apologize during their meal. The restaurant uses a sophisticated AI system that monitors conversations and counts apologies in real-time. Each 'sorry' costs $5, with the first 3 apologies being free as a 'courtesy allowance.' Customers who exceed 20 apologies during a single meal eat for free, as the restaurant considers them 'too polite to charge.' Early customers report saying 'sorry' an average of 34 times per meal, with one couple apologizing to each other 47 times over appetizers alone. The restaurant's most common apologies include 'sorry, is this seat taken? Oh wait, we're seated already, sorry' and 'sorry, could I bother you for some water? Sorry.' The concept has been so successful that a chain is planned for 2026.",
      "category": "lifestyle",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/177.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Restaurant interior with apology counter above counter, waiters holding up apology tally, customers saying sorry, comic illustration, flat design, blue and yellow colors",
      "publishedAt": "1 day ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 81,
      "tags": ["Lifestyle", "Restaurant", "Apology", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "178",
      "headline": "Company Introduces 'Meeting-Free Wednesdays' Then Fills Them With 'Sync-Ups' Which Are Just Meetings",
      "summary": "The 'Creative Communication Collaboration Session' is a 3-hour meeting that could have been an email.",
      "fullBody": "MegaCorp Industries has announced 'Meeting-Free Wednesdays' to improve productivity, then immediately filled the day with 8 hours of 'Creative Communication Collaboration Sessions,' 'Strategic Alignment Sync-Ups,' and 'Cross-Functional Touchpoint Discussions.' One employee calculated that the new meeting-free day contains 47 meetings, totaling 11 hours of continuous talking about talking. The company's internal memo stated, 'We've eliminated traditional meetings and replaced them with innovative conversation formats that are definitely not meetings.' Employees report that they now do their actual work on Thursdays and Fridays, which were previously 'meeting-heavy days.' HR says the change has been 'transformative for collaboration,' while employees say it has been 'transformative for exhaustion.'",
      "category": "lifestyle",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/178.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Calendar showing MEETING FREE WEDNESDAY with 47 meetings underneath, employee looking defeated at screen, comic illustration, flat design, gray and red colors",
      "publishedAt": "2 days ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 81,
      "tags": ["Lifestyle", "Meetings", "Work", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "179",
      "headline": "New Study Finds That 'I'll Start Tomorrow' Is Humanity's Most Popular Procrastination Technique",
      "summary": "Researchers found that 47% of 'tomorrows' never actually arrive, they just get rescheduled to the next tomorrow.",
      "fullBody": "A comprehensive study from the Institute of Perpetual Delay has confirmed that 'I'll start tomorrow' is humanity's most popular procrastination technique and that 47% of 'tomorrows' never actually arrive. Researchers followed 1,000 people who said they'd 'start tomorrow' on various goals and found that 'tomorrow' kept getting pushed back until it became 'next week,' then 'next month,' then 'when I feel ready,' which was never. The lead researcher said, 'Tomorrow is a fictional place where good intentions go to die. People keep scheduling things for tomorrow, but tomorrow keeps not arriving in a form that's useful.' Participants reported saying 'I'll start tomorrow' an average of 47 times before giving up entirely. The institute recommends 'starting today or accepting you'll never start.'",
      "category": "science",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/179.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Calendar pages labeled TOMORROW stacked up, person looking at them with confused expression, tomorrow never arriving, comic illustration, flat design, purple and gray colors",
      "publishedAt": "2 days ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 78,
      "tags": ["Science", "Study", "Procrastination", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "180",
      "headline": "New Research Shows That 'I'm Not Into That' Really Means 'I've Never Actually Tried It'",
      "summary": "Scientists found that 90% of strong opinions about not liking things come from people who've never actually experienced them.",
      "fullBody": "Cultural researchers at the Institute of Questionable Opinions have discovered that 'I'm not into that' really means 'I've never actually tried it but I've decided I don't like it based on nothing.' The study found that 90% of people who say they don't like certain foods, activities, or experiences have never actually tried them. One participant said, 'I told people I hated sushi for 47 years, then I finally tried it and now I eat it every week. I wasted so much time being wrong.' Researchers found that people often develop strong negative opinions about things to 'fit in' or 'belong to a group' without actually experiencing the thing they're dismissing. The study recommends 'trying things before having opinions about them' but notes that 'people will still say they're not into things they've never tried because that's easier than admitting they might be wrong.'",
      "category": "science",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/180.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Person saying I'M NOT INTO THAT while sushi plate sits untouched in front of them, other person pointing at plate saying HAVE YOU EVEN TRIED IT, comic illustration, flat design, red and gray colors",
      "publishedAt": "2 days ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 78,
      "tags": ["Science", "Research", "Opinions", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "181",
      "headline": "Scientists Confirm That 'I Was Almost There' Actually Means 'I Got Lost And Won't Admit It'",
      "summary": "The study found that 'almost there' is code for 'I may have passed your house 3 times already.'",
      "fullBody": "Navigation researchers at the Institute of Questionable Direction Skills have confirmed that 'I was almost there' actually means 'I got lost and won't admit it.' The study found that people who say 'I was almost there' have often passed the destination 2-3 times, made unauthorized U-turns, and ignored their GPS while saying 'I know a shortcut.' One participant said, 'I told my friend I was almost there when I was actually in the next town over, lost, and too proud to admit I needed help.' Researchers found that 'almost there' is a way of saving face while admitting you have no idea where you are. The study recommends 'just admitting you're lost' but notes that 'pride prevents this 90% of the time.' The institute is developing an app that automatically texts your location to people when you're lost.",
      "category": "science",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/181.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Person in car passing same building 3 times saying I WAS ALMOST THERE, map in GPS showing zigzag path, other person watching from window confused, comic illustration, flat design, blue and gray colors",
      "publishedAt": "2 days ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 75,
      "tags": ["Science", "Study", "Navigation", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "182",
      "headline": "New Study Finds That 'I'll Do It Later' Actually Means 'I Will Never Do It And Feel Guilty Forever'",
      "summary": "Researchers confirmed that 'later' is a fictional time that exists only in the imagination of procrastinators.",
      "fullBody": "Psychological researchers at the Institute of Perpetual Intent have confirmed that 'I'll do it later' actually means 'I will never do it and feel guilty about it for the rest of my life.' The study found that 'later' is a fictional time that exists only in the imagination of procrastinators and has never actually arrived for anyone in recorded history. One researcher said, 'Later is the time of good intentions and broken promises. People keep scheduling things for later, but later keeps not coming. It's basically a lie we tell ourselves.' The study followed people who said 'I'll do it later' and found that 90% of those tasks were never completed, 7% were eventually completed by someone else, and 3% were completed 47 years later by accident. The institute recommends 'just doing it now' but acknowledges this is 'easier said than done.'",
      "category": "science",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/182.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Person scheduling task for LATER, calendar showing LATER never arriving, ghost of unfinished task haunting them, comic illustration, flat design, gray and purple colors",
      "publishedAt": "3 days ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 77,
      "tags": ["Science", "Study", "Procrastination", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "183",
      "headline": "Scientists Confirm That 'I'm Not Jealous' Means 'I Am Extremely Jealous And This Is A Defense Mechanism'",
      "summary": "The study found that 'not jealous' correlates 100% with 'just saw your promotion on LinkedIn.'",
      "fullBody": "Emotional researchers at the Institute of Questionable Feelings have confirmed that 'I'm not jealous' means 'I am extremely jealous and this is a defense mechanism.' The study found that people who say 'I'm not jealous' often send a text immediately after seeing their ex's vacation photos, their coworker's promotion, or their friend's new relationship. One participant said, 'I'm definitely not jealous that you got promoted, I'm so happy for you, really.' Researchers observed that the 'really' at the end of that sentence was the tell. The study found that 'not jealous' is often followed by 'but how did you get that before me?' which proves the point. Researchers recommend 'just admitting you're jealous because it's a normal human emotion' but note that 'pride prevents this 100% of the time.'",
      "category": "science",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-10/183.jpg",
      "imagePrompt": "Person saying I'M NOT JEALOUS while green tint spreads over body, coworker celebrating in background, speech bubble saying BUT HOW DID YOU GET THAT, comic illustration, flat design, green and gray colors",
      "publishedAt": "3 days ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 79,
      "tags": ["Science", "Study", "Jealousy", "Satire"],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
