      "id": "193",
      "headline": "Scientists Confirm That 'I Was Almost There' Actually Means 'I Got Lost And Won't Admit It'",
      "summary": "The study found that 'almost there' is code for 'I may have passed your house 3 times already.'",
      "fullBody": "Navigation researchers at the Institute of Questionable Direction Skills have confirmed that 'I was almost there' actually means 'I got lost and won't admit it.' The study found that people who say 'I was almost there' have often passed the destination 2-3 times, made unauthorized U-turns, and ignored their GPS while saying 'I know a shortcut.' One participant said, 'I told my friend I was almost there when I was actually in the next town over, lost, and too proud to admit I needed help.' Researchers found that 'almost there' is a way of saving face while admitting you have no idea where you are. The study recommends 'just admitting you're lost' but notes that 'pride prevents this 90% of the time.' The institute is developing an app that automatically texts your location to people when you're lost.",
